    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it, so it's mostly just legal stuff and government backing. but it still feels weird to have all that money just sitting in some random company's hands instead of just being handled by the buyer and seller directly. i guess i'm just worried about the \"neutral\" part... like, what if the agent is tempted to do something suspicious with the stash? is there ever a situation where a third party holding assets is actually a bad thing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You are right that the \"trust\" aspect is the biggest part of the concern, but licensed escrow agents are legally restricted from commingling funds and are regularly audited, making their actions a matter of public record that you can verify. While third-party holding carries some inherent friction and cost, it is generally far safer and more compliant than trying to transact directly with one another without oversight, especially for complex loans. If a seller loses trust, they can often replace an agent mid-closing, which shows the system is designed to protect everyone if issues arise.",
